{"id": "60cada21-c1e6-4de9-85a3-06b56f4189a9", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T06:12:19.024893", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "75dcaf8c-acbe-498d-a582-b6a24684ad0f", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-bb7eacf8-3b42-445c-bc29-d2e3c32acd3a", "tool_call": {"id": "call-bb7eacf8-3b42-445c-bc29-d2e3c32acd3a", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Submit plan stage for fused Adam graph comparison\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## Plan Stage — Proposed Approach (Design-Only)\\n\\n### 1. Concise approach\\nCompare two **optimizer-step computational graphs** captured with TorchViz's `make_dot`, one built from a standard PyTorch Adam step and one built from TorchOpt's fused Adam step, under a single shared model/loss/input/seed. Quantify graph complexity and interpretability through counts, structural metrics, and a rubric over operator names. No execution is performed; the deliverable is the design document plus feasibility checks.\\n\\n### 2. Two configurations to compare\\n\\n**Config A — Baseline (TorchViz-style graph of standard Adam).**\\n- Model `M`: small illustrative MLP (e.g., `Linear→ReLU→Linear→ReLU→Linear`) with reproducible init (fixed seed).\\n- Loss: `F.cross_entropy` on a fixed mini-batch `X`.\\n- Step 1: `loss.backward()` builds the *gradient* graph (what TorchViz renders by default on `loss` or on a chosen parameter's `.grad_fn`).\\n- Step 2: simulate the Adam update by keeping it out-of-graph (since `.step()` is in-place and breaks the graph), OR — preferred for direct comparison — realize Adam as a *pure functional* sequence of PyTorch ops on leaf tensors so the update remains in-graph and TorchViz can render the full loss→backward→update chain. This gives the \\\"TorchViz baseline graph\\\".\\n- Render with `torchviz.make_dot(loss_for_render, params=dict(M.named_parameters()), show_attrs=False, show_saved=False)`.\\n\\n**Config B — TorchOpt fused Adam (the treatment).**\\n- Same model, same loss, same seed.\\n- Use a TorchOpt differentiable/torch-friendly fused Adam API. The author's intent is the **fused-meta** path: build the optimizer step as a single functional transform such that the per-element substeps (m, v, m̂, v̂, θ update) collapse into one node-level op rather than ~7+ atomic ops per parameter tensor. Two candidate implementations are listed in §4 to be picked by the researcher:\\n  - **B1.** `torchopt.MetaAdam(net, lr=...).step(net, state)` — the meta-/functional flavor whose inner apply_updates fuses the element-wise update via `torchopt.apply_updates` / `scale_by_adam` semantics; TorchViz is called on the *loss with grads retained across the step* so the optimizer step stays in-graph.\\n  - **B2.** `torchopt.Adam(net, lr=..., use_accelerated_op=True)` (a fused-impl path if the installed TorchOpt version exposes such a flag, e.g., via the accelerated optimizer backend) — produces a single op-name node for the update.\\n- Render with the same `torchviz.make_dot(...)` call as Config A on a non-in-place copy of the network.\\n\\n### 3. Metrics (operational definitions)\\n\\n**Complexity (lower is \\\"less complex\\\"):**\\n- `N_nodes` = count of `Digraph.node()` entries in the rendered dot.\\n- `N_edges` = count of `Digraph.edge()` entries.\\n- `N_op_kinds` = unique op-name roots (e.g., `AddmmBackward`, `MulBackward0`, `SqrtBackward`, `DivBackward0`).\\n- `Adam_subop_count` = number of nodes whose op-name is in `{Add, Mul, Sub, Div, Sqrt, Addcmul, Pow, Norm}` (the typical element-wise Adam atomics), restricted to nodes that lie *after* the gradient-producing backbone nodes (a topological cutoff at `AccumulateGrad` sinks).\\n- `graph_depth` = longest path length in the DAG (computed on `networkx.DiGraph` derived from the dot).\\n- `branching_stats` = mean/max out-degree.\\n\\n**Interpretability (higher is \\\"more interpretable\\\"):**\\n- A 0–5 rubric on the rendered dot, applied independently twice (or via a simple deterministic regex match) using these criteria:\\n  1. The five conceptual Adam phases (g, m, v, m̂, v̂, θ) are visually identifiable as connected subgraphs.\\n  2. Single-node fused update present? (Boolean; +1 if at least one op node per parameter tensor carries a fused name like `ScaleByAdam`/`FusedAdam`/`AdamUpdate`.)\\n  3. Ratio of \\\"semantic\\\" nodes (named gradient flows involving parameter names) to total nodes ≥ some threshold.\\n  4. No more than `K` (e.g., 3) element-wise atomic op nodes per parameter tensor (indicating fusion reduced them).\\n  5. Graph can be summarized in plain English to a reader in ≤ N sentences without losing the Adam mechanics.\\n- An automated proxy of (1)–(4) is added: walk the dot with `networkx` and check label regexes for the canonical Adam phase tokens; report per-parameter \\\"atomic-op-density\\\".\\n\\n### 4. Controls and fixed variables\\n- Same architecture, init seed, batch `X`, `y`, loss, dtype (float32), device (CPU — GPU disabled).\\n- Same `torchviz.make_dot` settings (`show_attrs`, `show_saved`, `params` argument — pass `dict(M.named_parameters())` so parameter nodes are explicitly named).\\n- Same Graphviz engine (`dot`, `rankdir=LR`) if rendered to image; the design reports only on the dot graph structure, so engine choice does not bias the metrics.\\n- Identical rendering call site: graph built from the *same leaf* tensor (loss) so the autograd walk covers the same forward view where possible.\\n- Decision rule for evaluation: each metric is reported per parameter tensor (`W1, b1, W2, b2, W3, b3`) and aggregated by mean; per-tensor reporting guards against parameter-shape-driven differences.\\n\\n### 5. Feasibility checks (to record in the design)\\n- The bubblewrap/system-Python policy env precludes running `pip install torch torchviz torchopt graphviz networkx` and therefore precludes executing the experiment in this environment. The design explicitly names which artifacts would have to be produced offline (dot files, metric CSVs, rubric scores) and which Python/pip invocations would be required.\\n- A dry-run feasibility command is specified: `python -c \\\"import torch, torchviz, torchopt, graphviz, networkx; print('ok')\\\"` as a precondition that must succeed before any graph capture is attempted.\\n- A no-import sanity check `python -c \\\"import sys; print(sys.version)\\\"` plus an offline-cache presence check (e.g., `pip download --no-deps torch torchviz torchopt -d /workspace/vendor`) is suggested as a preflight if execution later becomes possible in a different sandbox.\\n\\n### 6. Consequential choices to lock in now vs defer to the researcher\\n- **Lock in:** (i) Two-config structure (baseline Adam vs TorchOpt fused Adam); (ii) TorchViz as the *graph producer* in both configs — ensures an apples-to-apples comparison; (iii) The metric list above; (iv) Off-by-default image rendering — the dot graph alone is the artifact.\\n- **Defer to researcher:** (i) Which TorchOpt Adam API specifically (B1 vs B2 in §2); (ii) Whether the optimizer step must remain differentiable/in-graph (a meta-update scenario) or only the pure-update graph matters; (iii) Whether to include a third config — explicit functional-but-not-fused Adam — as a \\\"fused vs functional, holding everything else equal\\\" reference; (iv) Whether the rubric should be a one-shot single-rater score or a between-rater agreement (Cohen's κ) — the latter adds a participant cost.\\n\\n### 7. Definition of a positive finding (\\\"fusing improves interpretability\\\")\\nThe proposed design concludes that TorchOpt's fused Adam produces a less complex / more interpretable gradient graph if **all** of the following hold in the captured dot graphs:\\n- `Adam_subop_count` per parameter ≥ 30% lower in B than A.\\n- A single canonical \\\"fused update\\\" node per parameter tensor appears in B, absent in A.\\n- The interpretability rubric reports ≥ +2 on the 0–5 scale in B vs A.\\n- `graph_depth` is reduced (i.e., not increased by adding a fusion wrapper).\\n\\nA *negative* or *null* finding (no statistically meaningful reduction in `Adam_subop_count`, or the \\\"fused\\\" node actually expands because the wrapper introduces extra bookkeeping nodes) is treated as an equally informative outcome — the design does not bias toward a positive result.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Awaiting researcher approval before I lift this to the full design (result stage).** Please confirm or amend: (a) the two-config A/B structure, (b) the choice between TorchOpt API flavors B1 (MetaAdam) vs B2 (accelerated fused Adam), (c) whether to add a \\\"functional-but-not-fused\\\" third config, and (d) any rubric adjustments.\",\"evidence\":\"Pre-task exploration of /workspace and its environment:\\n- /workspace contains only task.json (7 lines). No TorchOpt, TorchViz, PyTorch, networkx, graphviz, or any project dependencies present.\\n- Python 3.12.3 found at /bin/python3 (system Python). No GPU drivers relevant; bubblewrap env confirmed via path layout.\\n- pip3 list shows only OS-/system-level packages (asn1crypto, boto3, cloud-init, etc.). None of torch, torchviz, torchopt, graphviz, networkx are present.\\n- `python3 -c 'import torch'`, `'import torchviz'`, `'import graphviz'`, `'import networkx'` all raise ModuleNotFoundError.\\n- task.json documents.track.md states: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n- /home/actor empty; /tmp empty; no cached wheels.\\n- Network disabled (no apt/pip reach expected). Therefore, any execution would require wheel cache; design is treated as submission-ready markdown only.\\n- The question references two named libraries (TorchOpt for fused Adam, TorchViz for graph output). Both are external and absent — design cites their public APIs by name only (torchviz.make_dot; torchopt.MetaAdam / torchopt.apply_updates / functional fused Adam).\",\"assumptions\":\"- The researcher's \\\"TorchViz gradient graph\\\" refers to the output of `torchviz.make_dot` over a chosen tensor (loss or parameter `.grad_fn`), which is the PyTorch-community usage convention.\\n- \\\"Adam optimizer (as done in TorchOpt)\\\" refers to a functional / fused Adam path (i.e., `torchopt.MetaAdam` or equivalent `apply_updates`-based fused step), not a vanilla `torchopt.Adam` that mirrors stock PyTorch op-by-op.\\n- \\\"Less complex / more interpretable graph\\\" can be operationalized with structural counts (nodes, edges, op-kinds, atomic-op density, depth) plus a simple rubric on phase identifiability; more elaborate graph-theoretic metrics (entropy, modularity) are not assumed required.\\n- The bubblewrap/system-Python policy env is truly run-isolated; offline-only design submission is the correct deliverable.\\n- Network is disabled so no pip/wheel fetch is possible in this environment; design-only, no execution.\",\"uncertainties\":\"- Whether the TorchOpt API path should be (B1) `MetaAdam` differentiable step (optimizer stays in-graph, renderable by TorchViz) vs (B2) accelerated/utility fused Adam that may or may not preserve graph structure — affects whether \\\"the graph\\\" under TorchOpt is even renderable end-to-end. Researcher preference needed.\\n- Whether to include a third \\\"functional Adam but not fused\\\" control, which would isolate fusion's contribution from functionalization's contribution. Extra cost vs extra clarity.\\n- Whether to attach a human-rated rubric (and how many raters / how to score) versus relying solely on automated metric proxies. Researcher decides.\\n- Whether TorchViz call should target `loss`, a chosen parameter's `.grad_fn`, or the post-update parameter leaf — each gives a different \\\"gradient graph\\\" object. Researcher preference needed.\\n- Whether the depth metric should be computed via `networkx.from_dot_data` (which requires graphviz offline) or a manual Python DAG walk from the dot edges (pure stdlib) — the design prefers the latter to remain feasible offline.\\n- Reproducibility of `make_dot` output across PyTorch versions: dot-graph structure can shift with autograd graph refactors; the design should pin versions, but versions are not yet specified by the researcher.\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-e126c48d-4878-44cc-9704-412f2fda286f", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
